# Zelotes C-18 vertical mouse.
#
# A rebadge. The hardware identifies itself as manufacturer "SOAI", product
# "USB Gaming Mouse", no serial, bcdDevice 2.11 — nothing on the wire says
# Zelotes or C-18, so the presentable name has to come from here. Also sold
# as a Steren in Latin America. Upstream libratbag calls it "SOAI C18 Gaming
# Mouse"; rename if you would rather match.
#
# Driver=c18 matches the driver name in libratbag PR #1877. That driver does
# NOT exist here yet, so a matching device currently surfaces as
# `Error::UnknownDriver` — deliberate, and the documented behaviour of
# `DriverRegistry::with_builtin`. It makes the mouse *recognised* (ours, not
# yet drivable) rather than *unknown* (skipped silently).
#
# ## Prior art
#
# The protocol is reverse engineered and documented. Do not start from
# scratch.
#
#   libratbag/libratbag#1526   the support request, closed 2023 as
#                              "needs a new driver, no volunteer"
#   libratbag/libratbag#1877   OPEN, unmerged: a complete `driver-c18.c`
#                              plus `data/devices/soai-c18.device`. MIT,
#                              © 2026 Padre Adamo — portable into this
#                              project with attribution.
#   PadreAdamo/c18_reverseengineer
#                              PROTOCOL.md, ~1000 lines, from USBPcap
#                              captures of the vendor software. **No LICENSE
#                              file**, so treat it as documentation to read,
#                              not source to copy. Port from the MIT PR.
#   JoaoCarlosPereira/zelotes-mouse
#                              a Python control app, also unlicensed.
#
# ## The hardware
#
# Verified locally from the three report descriptors, and matching the lsusb
# dump in issue #1526 byte for byte:
#
#   iface 0  boot mouse (subclass 01, protocol 02). 5 buttons, 16-bit X/Y,
#            wheel, AC Pan. No report IDs. Motion only.
#   iface 1  keyboard (report 0x01), system control (0x02), consumer (0x03),
#            and a 3-byte Input-only vendor collection at report 0x06.
#   iface 2  the configuration channel. Usage page 0xFF00, *no report ID*:
#            64-byte Input, 64-byte Output, 8-byte Feature.
#
# All three nodes carry the same bus/vid/pid, so `DeviceMatch=` cannot pick
# interface 2 and probing must. Upstream's test is
# `usage_page(0) == 0xff00 && !has_report(1)`; writing to the wrong interface
# gives EPIPE/STALL.
#
# ## What the driver will have to do
#
# Capabilities: 2 profiles, 11 buttons, 5 DPI stages, 1 LED, macros up to 96
# keys. Report rates 125/250/500/1000 Hz. DPI 200–10000, set from a 16-point
# calibration table with linear interpolation between points — the mapping is
# not a formula. Per-axis DPI is not supported by the hardware at all.
#
# Transactions are always `0x01 begin` → payload → `0x02 select/commit`.
# Every 8-byte control packet ends in a checksum such that all eight bytes
# sum to 0xFF (`checksum = 0xFF - sum(bytes[0..=6])`).
#
# **Two transports with opposite conventions**, both established empirically
# with usbmon — this is the part to get right first:
#
#   * 8-byte control commands go out as a Feature report (HIDIOCSFEATURE).
#     The report is genuinely unnumbered, so byte 0 is the opcode itself and
#     must survive untouched. Prepending a 0x00 report-number byte gets an
#     immediate EPIPE/STALL. `HidrawDevice::set_feature_report` already
#     behaves correctly here: it copies the caller's slice verbatim and never
#     overwrites byte 0.
#   * 64-byte payloads go out as an Output report (plain `write(2)`), as
#     **65** bytes with a dummy 0x00 first. The kernel's hidraw write path
#     strips byte 0 only when it is literally 0x00, so a payload that really
#     begins with 0x00 — macro headers, zero ack packets — loses a real byte
#     and is silently corrupted unless the dummy is there.
#
# 20 ms between packets; 200 ms after macro writes.
#
# **There is no readback.** Nothing can be queried, so probe cannot discover
# current state and has to assume the defaults. That suits
# `DeviceBackend::reload`'s no-op default, which the trait docs describe as
# right for devices whose state libmouser fully owns.
#
# Access: data/udev/70-mouser-dev.rules covers this device; without it the
# nodes are root-only.

[Device]
Name=Zelotes C-18
DeviceMatch=usb:12c9:2003
DeviceType=mouse
Driver=c18
